var DEF = [
{z:"Office & Onshore",q:"Which statement best defines 'line of fire'?",o:["The painted boundary that separates a hazardous work area from a safe pedestrian zone","The line of people formed at the assembly point during an emergency evacuation drill","The reporting chain used to escalate an incident from site staff to senior management","The path a moving object, vehicle or released energy follows, injuring anybody in it"],a:3,e:"Line of fire is about the path of energy or movement and whether a person is in it. It is not a boundary, a queue or a reporting structure."},
{z:"Office & Onshore",q:"Your colleague pulls out the top two drawers of a tall filing cabinet at once to find a file. Which line-of-fire hazard has she just created?",o:["The drawer tracks can warp badly under the unsupported weight of the open drawers","The cabinet's centre of gravity shifts outward and it could tip forward onto her","The drawers can slam shut suddenly and trap her fingers inside the steel runners","The metal casing can build up static electricity and give her an electric shock"],a:1,e:"Opening multiple drawers shifts the centre of gravity. Tall cabinets should be anchored and only one drawer opened at a time."},
{z:"Office & Onshore",q:"Boxes of A4 paper are stacked five high on the top shelf of the stationery store, directly above the door. Which is the STRONGEST control?",o:["Move heavy items to lower shelves so nothing stays directly above anyone","Install a high-visibility hazard sign on the door to warn people entering","Brief everyone about the stacking risk so they enter the room cautiously","Require everyone to wear an approved hard hat when entering the storeroom"],a:0,e:"Elimination beats signs, briefings and PPE. If the object cannot fall onto a person, nobody has to rely on behaviour."},
{z:"Office & Onshore",q:"A 'Caution: Falling Objects' sign is placed under a badly stacked shelf. In the hierarchy of controls, this is:",o:["An engineering control, which physically prevents the items from falling off the shelves","An elimination control, which removes the falling object hazard from the workplace fully","An administrative control, which depends on human behaviours and is the weakest category","A personal protective equipment control, which guards the worker from big falling objects"],a:2,e:"Signs only warn. They do not stop an object from falling or move anyone out of the way."},
{z:"Office & Onshore",q:"You arrive at the office car park at 7:45am and every colleague is walking in at the same time. Which parking approach reduces line-of-fire risk the most?",o:["Reverse into the bay during a quiet interval, then drive forwards when leaving","Drive nose-in and rely on the reverse beeper to alert pedestrians when leaving","Drive nose-in because it reduces the time spent reversing during the early rush","Park diagonally across two bays to give pedestrians the most clearance around you"],a:0,e:"The riskiest time to reverse is at close of work, when the lot is full of people. Reversing in early shifts the manoeuvre to a low-traffic moment."},
{z:"Office & Onshore",q:"A pedestrian lane crosses the vehicle lane in the car park and the driver coming towards you is on a call. What is the best check before crossing?",o:["Cross quickly because pedestrians always keep the right of way over any vehicle","Wait for clear eye contact to confirm the driver acknowledged you and stopped","Listen attentively for the engine speeding up and proceed if you hear nothing","Hasten across the vehicle lane at a brisk speed, spending minimal time there"],a:1,e:"Right of way does not stop a vehicle. A clear acknowledgement from the driver does."},
{z:"Office & Onshore",q:"You are walking through the company supply base yard to the warehouse office wearing earbuds. What have the earbuds mainly removed?",o:["Your capacity to concentrate on the operational tasks you are about to complete","Your entitlement to take a designated rest break away from the central building","Your ability to hear backing vehicles and loud warnings of sudden threats nearby","Your compliance with normal office etiquette, unless you keep the sound quite low"],a:2,e:"Hearing is one of your main warning systems around mobile equipment."},
{z:"Office & Onshore",q:"You approach a double swing door with no vision panel at a busy corridor corner. Which habit is safest?",o:["Push the door open quickly so you spend less time near the hazard zone","Knock on the panel and wait for a verbal reply from the opposite side","Hold the door open with your elbow and body weight as you pass through","Open the door slowly and stand slightly to the side of its swing path"],a:3,e:"Blind doors are a classic struck-by hazard. Slow and to the side keeps your body out of the swing."},
{z:"Office & Onshore",q:"Moving a heavy printer on a trolley DOWN a ramp, where should you stand?",o:["Position yourself downhill in front of the load, ready to intercept it falling","Position yourself behind and uphill of the load, guiding its descent from above","Position yourself beside the ramp edge, gripping the leading corner to steer it","Position yourself underneath the ramp incline, with one hand on the trolley base"],a:1,e:"Never stand downhill of a load that can run away. If it slips you are in its path."},
{z:"Office & Onshore",q:"In the stores room, a gas cylinder has no valve cap and is not chained. What is the biggest line-of-fire concern?",o:["A broken valve lets escaping pressurised gas turn the cylinder into a fast projectile","The identification label can fade and cause a costly chemical handling mix-up later on","The cylinder can roll across the floor and generate an excessive noise hazard nearby","The exposed valve can attract debris and compromise the quality of the contained gas"],a:0,e:"A cylinder with a damaged valve can be propelled at high speed by escaping gas."},
{z:"Office & Onshore",q:"A facilities worker plans to remove the gas-lift cylinder of an old office chair by hitting it with a hammer. What is the hazard?",o:["The chair's adjustment mechanism will lose its original height setting after the impact","The hammer can leave permanent marks on the exterior aluminium cylinder casing surface","Pressurised cylinder contents and metal parts can be ejected outwards with great force","The impact could fracture the surrounding porcelain floor tiles if the chair collapses"],a:2,e:"Gas springs store energy. Opening or hammering them without the correct procedure can eject components."},
{z:"Office & Onshore",q:"A cleaner uses a compressed air hose to blow dust off her uniform. Which line-of-fire hazard is she exposing herself to?",o:["The pressure can shrink the uniform fabric fibres and badly damage the material itself","The uninterrupted airflow can collapse the hose and trigger a sudden loss of pressure","The hissing noise can trigger an immediate and severe tension headache for the cleaner","The air can penetrate skin, causing injection injuries, and throw debris into the eyes"],a:3,e:"Compressed air should never be pointed at the body. It can force air into the bloodstream and throw particles at high speed."},
{z:"Office & Onshore",q:"A contractor will replace ceiling panels and light fittings above the open-plan work area. Which control matters most?",o:["Instruct the team on the floor below to keep working with additional vigilance","Install a hard barricade to stop people from entering the area directly below","Place a high-visibility traffic cone next to the base of the worker's ladder","Require office staff to keep their heads down while passing through the area"],a:1,e:"If a tool or panel falls, nobody should be standing underneath. Exclusion beats awareness."},
{z:"Office & Onshore",q:"Window cleaners are working from a cradle on the 15th floor of your HQ. What should be in place at ground level?",o:["A clear warning sign positioned only at the main reception desk for all visitors","Nothing at all is required because the workers are tethered to the platform above","A fully barricaded safety zone at floor level, and bold hazard signs shown nearby","A security guard who tells pedestrians to hurry past the active work zone quickly"],a:2,e:"Tethering reduces dropped tools but does not eliminate it. The area below must be protected."},
{z:"Office & Onshore",q:"A new tall bookshelf has been delivered to the meeting room but is not fixed to the wall. What is the right action?",o:["Anchor it to the wall firmly, placing the heaviest items on bottom shelving","Place the heaviest items on top shelves to add downward pressure for balance","Leave it loose because the meeting room has very little foot traffic overall","Move it next to the entrance so that everyone can access items conveniently"],a:0,e:"Unanchored tall furniture can tip. Heavy items at the base lower the centre of gravity."},
{z:"Office & Onshore",q:"You feel an earth tremor while in the office. Which action avoids line-of-fire hazards best?",o:["Stand next to an exterior window to watch how the situation outside develops","Hurry to the nearest elevator and exit the building as quickly as possible","Stand by the tallest filing cabinet because it is the sturdiest item nearby","Move away from glazing and tall furniture, then take cover beneath a table"],a:3,e:"Glass and falling furniture are the main injury mechanisms in tremors."},
{z:"Office & Onshore",q:"On the way to the office, a colleague says, 'I am in the back seat, so I don't need a seatbelt, the front seats will protect me.' What is the flaw?",o:["There is no flaw here, because contemporary front seats are built to withstand all impacts","In a crash, an unbelted rear passenger is a heavy projectile hitting the front occupants","The only flaw is that skipping a rear seatbelt breaks traffic regulations in most places","The minor flaw is that rear seats are slightly more comfortable with the seatbelt fastened"],a:1,e:"An unrestrained person at 60 km/h can hit front occupants with several times their own body weight in force."},
{z:"Office & Onshore",q:"A colleague puts a laptop and a water bottle on the rear parcel shelf of the company car. What is the issue?",o:["The laptop's components can overheat fast from direct exposure to strong summer sun","The water bottle can leak onto the shelf upholstery and cause irreversible staining","In a sudden stop, they become high-speed projectiles aimed at the forward occupants","The items partially block the driver's view through the rear-view mirror and window"],a:2,e:"Loose items store kinetic energy and travel forward in a sudden stop."},
{z:"Office & Onshore",q:"You are driving behind a flatbed truck carrying pipes secured with a single strap. What is the safest decision?",o:["Increase following distance, avoid driving alongside, pass when safe, and flag the hazard","Tailgate directly behind the vehicle to utilise its slipstream and bypass the crosswinds","Accelerate to overtake quickly and stay in position directly alongside its loaded trailer","Keep your current speed and position because the licensed driver holds all responsibility"],a:0,e:"Unsecured loads can shift or fall. Distance buys time and keeps you out of the load's path."},
{z:"Office & Onshore",q:"During office refurbishment, a contractor uses a powder-actuated (nail) tool on a wall. Which approach is correct?",o:["Barricade only the operator's side because the tool has a very small danger range","Stand behind the operator to maintain an unobstructed line of sight to the work","Walk through the area normally because the tool is only used on inner partitions","Clear and cordon off both sides of the wall, because fasteners can pass through"],a:3,e:"Fasteners can penetrate thin walls or ricochet. The hazard exists on the other side too."},
{z:"Office & Onshore",q:"You must walk from the office block to the stores through the base yard where forklifts operate. What is the best practice?",o:["Take the most direct route by weaving carefully between the tall stacks of pallets","Follow closely behind an operating forklift to utilise the path that it has cleared","Use the signed, fully segregated pedestrian walkway for the full length of the trip","Cross the main transport areas only after the forklift driver clearly waves at you"],a:2,e:"Segregation is an engineering control. Shortcuts between stacks put you in blind spots."},
{z:"Office & Onshore",q:"An automatic sliding gate at the office entrance has started to close while you are halfway through. What is the lesson?",o:["Move fast through the closing gap since the safety sensor will reverse the gate","Never enter or linger in a closing gate's path; wait until it completely stops","Physically restrain the outermost edge of the gate with your hand to halt it","Turn sideways and just slip between the moving gate and the fixed concrete post"],a:1,e:"Caught-between hazards happen in seconds. Sensors can fail."},
{z:"Office & Onshore",q:"A colleague's laptop battery looks swollen and is warm while charging on a desk. What is the best action?",o:["Leave the laptop charging so that its inner battery cells can reset fully tonight","Press firmly on the swollen casing to assess how seriously the cells are damaged","Enclose the laptop inside a plastic bag and address the whole problem much later","Unplug if safe, move people away, place it on a non-flammable surface, and report"],a:3,e:"A swollen battery can vent or burst, throwing fragments and flame. Keep people away and report."},
{z:"Office & Onshore",q:"You have used the same photocopier for 10 years and decide to clear a paper jam with the cover open and the machine still powered. Which factor most increases the risk?",o:["Complacency, because high familiarity makes staff skip the usual energy lockout steps","Inadequate lighting, because dim overhead lights hide the internal rollers from view","Paper type, because heavier sheets generate extra friction inside the copier machine","Equipment brand, because this copier model lacks the internal guards fitted elsewhere"],a:0,e:"Many line-of-fire injuries occur during routine tasks where people feel comfortable."},
{z:"Office & Onshore",q:"In your office, who is responsible for identifying line-of-fire hazards?",o:["Every employee, and each person has the authority to halt dangerous tasks","Only the Health and Safety department personnel on duty during that shift","Only the line supervisor responsible for the operational area at that time","Only the contractor staff who are carrying out the risky maintenance work"],a:0,e:"Hazards are noticed by those closest to the task. Everyone owns the observation and the action."},
{z:"Office & Onshore",q:"A staff shuttle bus is reversing in the car park. Where is the safest place to stand?",o:["Right behind the rear bumper, giving clear help to the onboard reverse camera system","Safely clear of the vehicle's path, visible to the driver, preferably with a banksman","Within the blind spot alongside the rear wheels, monitoring the clearance of the tyres","Close behind the exhaust pipe, waving your arms to attract the driver's full attention"],a:1,e:"Big vehicles have large blind spots. 'Guiding' while standing behind a reversing vehicle puts you in the worst spot."},
{z:"Office & Onshore",q:"A pedestal fan in the open-plan office has its guard removed because 'it kept rattling'. What is the hazard?",o:["Louder noise caused by unbalanced airflow moving across the whole open office room","Quicker accumulation of airborne dust on the uncovered surfaces of the fan blades","Unprotected exposure to rotating blades, and the danger of a blade being expelled","Higher electrical consumption as the motor adjusts for the missing front fan guard"],a:2,e:"Guards protect from rotating parts and from fragments if a blade fails."},
{z:"Office & Onshore",q:"You notice that a ceiling-mounted air-conditioning unit above your desk is sagging and its bracket is rusty. What do you do?",o:["Carry on with your ordinary office tasks until the unit displays signs of failing","Wait for the next routine service inspection to formally flag the issue to managers","Switch off the cooling function at the wall and keep sitting underneath the unit","Move from underneath the unit, restrict access to the area, and report the hazard"],a:3,e:"Visible deterioration above people is an immediate hazard to resolve."},
{z:"Office & Onshore",q:"A heavy binder falls off a shelf and misses you by one metre. You laugh it off. What is the best response?",o:["Dismiss the event since the binder missed you and nobody was badly hurt","Relocate the binder to a higher shelf so colleagues can walk past freely","Report the event as a near miss and repair the underlying storage issue","Tell your colleagues about the lucky escape and carry on with your day"],a:2,e:"The only difference between a near miss and an injury is the position of a person. Reporting drives prevention."},
{z:"Office & Onshore",q:"You are pushing an office-move trolley loaded with boxes stacked higher than your eyes along a busy corridor. What is best?",o:["Shout loud verbal warnings so approaching staff move fully out of your way","Speed up your walking pace to get through the crowded corridor area quickly","Wear a high-visibility vest so oncoming people can easily see your full shape","Decrease the stack height or employ a banksman so you can observe properly"],a:3,e:"If you cannot see the path, you cannot avoid the line of fire. Fix the visibility."},
{z:"Lifting, Cargo & Transfers",q:"During a crane lift on the platform, where should the banksman/signaller stand?",o:["Close to the suspended load, ready to steady it during unexpected strong winds","Beyond the swing arc, in eye contact, never trapped between load and structure","Between the moving load and the perimeter handrail to guide the final placement","Right beneath the lifted boom to give the crane operator the maximum visibility"],a:1,e:"A fixed structure behind you removes your escape route if the load swings."},
{z:"Lifting, Cargo & Transfers",q:"Which statement about tag lines is correct?",o:["Use a line long enough to stay clear, never wrap it around your fingers","Wrap the tag line around your dominant hand to keep a firm, secure grip","Keep the tag line as short as possible to boost your leverage over loads","Tie the tag line to the adjacent handrail to stop the load from rotating"],a:0,e:"A wrapped line can drag you along with the load or against a structure."},
{z:"Lifting, Cargo & Transfers",q:"The crane operator says, 'The load will only be up for 10 seconds, so it is fine to walk underneath.' What is correct?",o:["This is acceptable provided the suspended load is well below the permitted limit","Never walk beneath a hanging load, irrespective of how momentary the exposure is","This is acceptable provided the pedestrian is wearing a fully rated safety helmet","This is acceptable provided the banksman clearly approves the lift move in advance"],a:1,e:"The seconds you are under it are the seconds a failure becomes fatal."},
{z:"Lifting, Cargo & Transfers",q:"In rigging terms, what does standing 'in the bight' mean?",o:["Standing next to the primary hoisting hook of an operating large pedestal crane","Standing in the shadow cast by the raised crane boom during daylight operations","Standing on the outer seaward side of the deck handrail during cargo transfers","Positioned within the loop of a tensioned rope or cabling which could constrict"],a:3,e:"Bights close rapidly when tension changes. They are common sites of caught-in injuries."},
{z:"Lifting, Cargo & Transfers",q:"A supply vessel is moored and a tensioned mooring line suddenly parts. Which area is most dangerous?",o:["The pedestrian walkway located just aft of the vessel's bridge wing structure","The inner transit halls set deep within the accommodation and utility modules","The snap-back zone, which includes the line's rebound path and its extensions","The elevated operating platform positioned next to the aft crane control cabin"],a:2,e:"A parted line recoils with great energy in the direction of its length."},
{z:"Lifting, Cargo & Transfers",q:"Lifting a container from a supply vessel in a moving sea, which is the riskiest moment?",o:["The moment of lift-off, when heave can snatch and swing the load","The phase when the suspended load is raised high above the deck","The early phase when the crane hook is slack and fully unweighted","The standby phase when the crane boom is stationary and locked out"],a:0,e:"Vessel heave can snatch the load and overload the crane or swing the load."},
{z:"Lifting, Cargo & Transfers",q:"When landing a load on deck, where should hands and body be?",o:["Directly under the descending load to judge the exact moment of touchdown","Entirely outside the crush zone, using tag lines or approved push sticks","Between the falling load and the perimeter handrail to guide the corners","On top of the descending load, applying downward pressure to stabilise it"],a:1,e:"Crush and pinch points are created exactly where the load lands."},
{z:"Lifting, Cargo & Transfers",q:"A cargo container has been in transit on a rough crossing and you must open its doors. What is a wise precaution?",o:["Stand right in front of the doors to carry out an instant visual inspection","Unlatch and open both heavy doors at once to clear the staging area quickly","Stand to the side, open the doors carefully, and expect shifted cargo to fall","Instruct a younger colleague to brace the cargo with their hands as doors open"],a:2,e:"Movement at sea can compress or lean cargo against the doors."},
{z:"Lifting, Cargo & Transfers",q:"On a supply vessel, gas cylinders are stored in a rack and the retaining bar is left unlatched in rough weather. What is the hazard?",o:["Unsecured cylinders may roll fiercely, and a ruptured valve makes a high-speed missile","Saltwater spray would accelerate the oxidation of the steel casing of every cylinder","Cylinders may rub together, making the standard outer colour coding illegible over time","Rolling cylinders will generate noise that causes minor annoyance to the working crew"],a:0,e:"Unsecured cylinders become both crushing and projectile hazards."},
{z:"Lifting, Cargo & Transfers",q:"A forklift is raising a pallet on the platform laydown area. Where should a pedestrian NOT be?",o:["Standing stationary at a safe distance, behind a painted demarcation boundary on deck","Walking along the designated pedestrian walkway, behind the steel barrier at the edge","Sitting safely inside the sealed control room while the main lifting activity proceeds","Standing anywhere beneath the raised forks, or anywhere within the mast's forward path"],a:3,e:"Raised loads and forks create a falling and crushing zone."},
{z:"Lifting, Cargo & Transfers",q:"You are being transferred to a platform in a personnel basket. Which is the right practice?",o:["Keep every limb within the basket edge and never press against the structures","Extend your free arm outward to fend off the nearing steel platform structure","Grip the extreme outside edge of the basket rim for maximum physical stability","Stand on the outside ring and brace your legs against the adjacent structure"],a:0,e:"The basket can swing and pin limbs between it and a structure."},
{z:"Lifting, Cargo & Transfers",q:"A crane is about to lift, and a worker on the deck sees a person in the lift path and shouts 'Stop!' but the banksman did not see him. What should the crane operator do?",o:["Pause all work briefly until the banksman clearly confirms the stop call first","Suspend the lift immediately, because a stop signal from anyone must be obeyed","Carry on lifting, because the command did not come from the assigned banksman","Raise the hoisting speed to clear the contested area before an incident occurs"],a:1,e:"A stop signal from anyone must be obeyed by everyone."},
{z:"Drilling & Well Operations",q:"A joint of drill pipe is being picked up from the catwalk to the V-door. Which is the most dangerous place to stand?",o:["Positioned next to the driller's control cabin at the perimeter of the pipe deck","Positioned behind a solid barricade set well away from the active pipe deck area","Positioned on the high driller's console platform, with a full view of the deck","Positioned in line with the extremities of the pipe, or within its swinging arc"],a:3,e:"The pipe can swing, roll or kick out at either end."},
{z:"Drilling & Well Operations",q:"Breaking out a connection with rig tongs, where should you stand?",o:["Positioned next to the tong handle to add manual pushing force during the bite","Positioned behind the tong handle while keeping a firm grip on the tension line","Positioned outside the swing arc of the tong handle and the taut breakout line","Positioned directly ahead of the tong to supervise how the dies bite the pipe"],a:2,e:"If the line parts or the tong slips it swings across the arc with great force."},
{z:"Drilling & Well Operations",q:"An iron roughneck is spinning and torquing a connection. Where must your hands be?",o:["Positioned clear of moving parts, with zero energy proven before using the unit","Positioned immediately beside the jaws, so you can guide the pipe into alignment","Positioned directly on the exterior of the rotating pipe to measure the torque","Positioned between the machine arms to brush away the metal debris building up"],a:0,e:"Never place hands in an area where machinery can move."},
{z:"Drilling & Well Operations",q:"A driller is wearing a loose coverall sleeve and cleaning a pump near a rotating shaft. Which hazard is created?",o:["A minor static shock triggered by friction between the clothing and metal casing","Mechanical entanglement, which can pull a limb or the torso into the machinery","Gradual hearing loss caused by extended exposure to the noise of loud equipment","Raised heat stress caused by hard physical work near the hot running equipment"],a:1,e:"Rotating shafts grab loose clothing instantly."},
{z:"Drilling & Well Operations",q:"Why are whip checks (safety clamps/cables) fitted at hose couplings of high-pressure hoses?",o:["To provide a colour-coded marker that indicates which fluid is within the hose","To add more weight that holds the hose firm during high-speed pumping operations","To hold the hose if a high-pressure coupling fails, stopping it whipping wildly","To provide an additional seal that prevents small fluid leaks at the connection"],a:2,e:"A failed coupling on a pressurised hose can whip violently."},
{z:"Drilling & Well Operations",q:"A hammer union on a high-pressure line is leaking slightly. A colleague suggests tightening it with a hammer while the line is pressurised. What is correct?",o:["Strike the union gently, because a small leak means there is minimal pressure","Strike the union with a non-sparking brass hammer to eliminate any ignition risk","Strike the union firmly while you stand behind the rigid main piping structure","Never strike a live union; depressurise fully and verify zero energy before work"],a:3,e:"A pressurised union can separate violently when struck."},
{z:"Drilling & Well Operations",q:"Before working on a mud pump discharge line, what is the correct step?",o:["Close the discharge valve and loosen the flange connection quickly to save time","Ask the pump operator to reduce the equipment's operating speed before you start","Isolate the system, depressurise to zero, confirm on a gauge, then stand clear","Loosen all the fastening bolts gradually while standing in front of the flange"],a:2,e:"A closed valve does not mean zero pressure; trapped pressure may remain."},
{z:"Drilling & Well Operations",q:"BOP pressure testing is about to start. Who should be in the area?",o:["Any staff who have a passing interest in observing the critical testing procedure","Off-duty crew members who are enjoying their scheduled rest breaks in the vicinity","Any people, provided they are wearing mandatory high-impact hard hats at all times","Only essential personnel, with barricades in place and all others kept well clear"],a:3,e:"Pressure tests store a lot of energy. Keep the area clear."},
{z:"Drilling & Well Operations",q:"During slickline or wireline operations, which is the greatest line-of-fire hazard?",o:["The constant noise made by the wireline spooling over the overhead sheaves","The tensioned wire snapping suddenly and whipping across the open work area","The chemical composition of the outer paint on the metal lubricator exterior","The progressive accumulation of heat leaving the wireline warm to the touch"],a:1,e:"Parted wire recoils with force and can cut. Keep out of its path and the sheave area."},
{z:"Drilling & Well Operations",q:"Perforating guns are being armed on the rig floor. Which control is essential?",o:["Restrict access, enforce radio silence, and never point guns at any personnel","Allow all off-duty crew to watch the procedure for practical training purposes","Use standard mobile phones to coordinate the arming sequence from a distance","Direct the activated devices towards the ocean so nobody onboard is endangered"],a:0,e:"An accidental firing sends a high-energy jet; people must be out of its path and electrical sources controlled."},
{z:"Drilling & Well Operations",q:"A logging tool containing a radioactive source is on the rig floor. What is the best way to reduce exposure?",o:["Stand close for an extremely short duration to minimise your overall exposure time","Depend on a certified hard hat and standard coveralls to block the radioactivity","Stand beside the source to keep constant visual oversight of the whole operation","Maximise distance, reduce time, use lead shields, and avoid the direct beam path"],a:3,e:"Radiation follows the line of emission. Time, distance and shielding are the key controls."},
{z:"Drilling & Well Operations",q:"A derrickman is racking pipe at the monkey board. Which is the correct way to carry hand tools?",o:["Tether every tool to your harness or a structure to prevent dropped objects","Put all small untethered hand tools in the breast pocket of your coveralls","Rest the tools on the elevated working platform and let gravity hold them","Throw the equipment down to the rig floor personnel underneath to save time"],a:0,e:"Dropped tools from height are a major line-of-fire hazard for the people below."},
{z:"Drilling & Well Operations",q:"Which is the best example of ELIMINATING the line-of-fire risk on a drill floor?",o:["Installing illuminated warning signs around the whole perimeter of the dangerous red zone","Using robotic pipe-handling machines so personnel are removed from the hazard zone fully","Holding a comprehensive verbal safety briefing with all personnel just before the shift","Requiring reinforced safety equipment for everyone working inside the zone at that time"],a:1,e:"If no one needs to be in the zone, no one can be hit."},
{z:"Drilling & Well Operations",q:"The rig floor has a marked 'red zone' near the pipe-handling equipment. What does it mean?",o:["A temporary laydown area set aside for storing special maintenance tools and equipment","An exclusive staging area where only senior supervisory personnel are permitted to stand","A restricted exclusion zone where moving machinery or released energy could cause harm","A designated recreational area where crew may gather safely during their planned breaks"],a:2,e:"Red zones identify where harm could occur from moving equipment or releases of energy."},
{z:"Pressure & Stored Energy",q:"You are breaking a flange that has been isolated. Which approach is the best?",o:["Quickly loosen the bolts closest to your position so the job completes sooner","Remove all the bolts from the assembly before checking for any trapped pressure","Slowly loosen the bolts furthest from your body so residual pressure vents away","Stand directly in line with the flange opening to observe the initial discharge"],a:2,e:"If trapped pressure remains, it should escape away from the worker."},
{z:"Pressure & Stored Energy",q:"You suspect a small pinhole leak on a high-pressure gas line. How do you find it safely?",o:["Slide a gloved hand along the outside of the pipe to feel pressure","Employ a gas detector or leak fluid, and keep every body part away","Hold a light piece of paper near the suspected leak to see movement","Put your ear very close to the leaking pipe section to hear hissing"],a:1,e:"A high-pressure jet can inject gas or fluid through the skin or cut like a blade."},
{z:"Pressure & Stored Energy",q:"Before opening the closure door of a pig receiver, what should you do?",o:["Verify the system is depressurised and drained, and stand safely to the side","Open the closure door the instant the digital pig arrival signal first fires","Remain directly in front of the closure door to ascertain the pig arrived","Disengage the door lock and remain in line with the receiver for clearance"],a:0,e:"Residual pressure can eject the door or the pig. Closure doors are a classic fatal line-of-fire hazard."},
{z:"Pressure & Stored Energy",q:"A pressure safety valve (PSV) lifts unexpectedly on the process deck. What is the line-of-fire hazard?",o:["The industrial paintwork on the exterior of the pressure safety valve flaking off","The temporary noise caused by the mechanical operation of the pressure safety valve","The colour coding of the valve mechanism being obscured by escaping process fluid","The discharge path, where a fast release of hazardous fluid strikes nearby people"],a:3,e:"PSVs discharge at high velocity and must be routed away from people."},
{z:"Pressure & Stored Energy",q:"A line is isolated by two closed valves with a bleed between them (double block and bleed). What must you still do before breaking containment?",o:["Start the task right away, because double valve isolation ensures safe conditions","Open the intermediate bleed valve between them and verify pressure registers zero","Depend on the shift handover notes stating the system was already depressurised","Visually check the upstream isolation valve position and skip the downstream one"],a:1,e:"Valves can leak by. Verify, do not assume."},
{z:"Pressure & Stored Energy",q:"A pressure gauge on a high-pressure line has a blow-out back. Why is this important?",o:["It reduces the production complexity and the total purchase cost of the device","It improves the visual sharpness and readability of the display for the operator","It removes the need for any regular calibration of the pressure instrument fully","If it ruptures, pressure escapes harmlessly to the rear, away from the observer"],a:3,e:"A designed blow-out path protects the person reading the gauge."},
{z:"Pressure & Stored Energy",q:"A worker uses a high-pressure water jetting gun. Which practice is correct?",o:["Maintain the nozzle pointed at the work surface and extremities clear of the jet","Test the pressure first by aiming the jet briefly against your own safety boot","Hold the trigger with one hand while pointing the nozzle at a nearby colleague","Bypass the dead-man handle so that the work is completed faster each working day"],a:0,e:"Water at jetting pressures can cut flesh and bone."},
{z:"Pressure & Stored Energy",q:"Why must compressed air hoses have whip checks?",o:["To act as a visible deterrent that stops pneumatic equipment being misplaced","To optimise the volumetric flow rate and conserve the compressed air supply","To hold a detached high-pressure hose and stop it whipping around violently","To elevate the hose above the deck and keep air-powered connectors spotless"],a:2,e:"Even air hoses can whip and cause serious injury when a coupling fails."},
{z:"Pressure & Stored Energy",q:"A hydraulic cylinder is holding up a heavy hatch cover. You must disconnect a hydraulic hose on that cylinder. What is the safest approach?",o:["Detach the live hose quickly while standing directly under the suspended hatch cover","Assume the internal hydraulic check valves will hold the load in position indefinitely","Ask an adjacent colleague to physically support the massive steel hatch cover manually","Fit an approved mechanical prop to hold the hatch before releasing hydraulic pressure"],a:3,e:"Hydraulic systems hold loads by pressure; releasing the fluid can drop the load."},
{z:"Pressure & Stored Energy",q:"A spring-return valve actuator is being dismantled. What energy source is still present even when the plant is shut down?",o:["Mechanical energy still held in the compressed inner steel return spring components","Residual high-voltage electrical energy stored in the local site power supply wiring","Residual thermal energy retained within the metallic components of the valve housing","No hazardous energy remains once the facility control systems are fully deactivated"],a:0,e:"Springs hold stored energy and can eject parts. Follow the manufacturer's de-energising procedure."},
{z:"Pressure & Stored Energy",q:"A hydraulic torque wrench or bolt tensioner is in use on a flange. Where should you keep your hands and body?",o:["Positioned very close to the reaction point to steady the heavy vibrating equipment","Positioned clear of the reaction arm and rotating parts, outside the twisting path","Positioned with one hand sandwiched between the reaction arm and the steel flange","Positioned behind the tool to keep continuous visual alignment with the target bolt"],a:1,e:"If the reaction arm slips, it can crush anything in its path."},
{z:"Pressure & Stored Energy",q:"During a pressure test, what is the correct way to raise pressure?",o:["Increase pressure immediately to the maximum test level to speed up the testing schedule","Increase pressure as fast as the equipment permits while staff monitor the gauge closely","Raise pressure in slight increments, checking integrity while staff stay out of the zone","Raise pressure in increments while the foreman holds the flexible hose to feel vibration"],a:2,e:"Staged increases allow leaks to be found before reaching maximum stored energy."},
{z:"Pressure & Stored Energy",q:"A standby pump is not running. Do you need to isolate it before maintenance?",o:["No, isolation is unnecessary because the pump is currently verified as already switched off","No, isolation is unnecessary provided you first tell the control room operator your plans","Yes, full isolation and lockout are mandatory because idle pumps can start without warning","Only if the pump exceeds a specified nominal volumetric flow capacity under company rules"],a:2,e:"Standby equipment can start automatically on a process signal."},
{z:"Pressure & Stored Energy",q:"After locking out equipment, what is the final step before starting work?",o:["Verify zero energy by attempting to start the machine and monitoring the system gauges","Assume the equipment holds zero stored energy because the padlock has been duly applied","Ask a separate colleague to confirm the isolation status by telephone from the office","Fit a second heavy-duty padlock to the main isolating device for added visual assurance"],a:0,e:"Locks isolate. Only testing proves energy has gone."},
{z:"Dropped Objects",q:"A 1 kg spanner falls 30 metres from the derrick. Roughly how fast is it going when it reaches the deck (ignoring drag)?",o:["It reaches roughly 10 kilometres per hour on impact, a harmless velocity","It reaches roughly 85-90 kilometres per hour on impact, a lethal velocity","It reaches roughly 30 kilometres per hour on impact, a moderate velocity","It reaches roughly 250 kilometres per hour on impact, an extreme velocity"],a:1,e:"v = sqrt(2 x 9.81 x 30), which is about 24 m/s or roughly 87 km/h. A small tool becomes a high-energy projectile."},
{z:"Dropped Objects",q:"A hard hat is rated for impacts. Does it guarantee protection from a 1 kg tool dropped from 30 metres?",o:["Yes, full protection is assured in all conditions if the helmet is worn correctly","Yes, full protection is guaranteed as long as the chin strap is tightened securely","Yes, full protection is guaranteed as long as the headgear was produced this year","No, hard hats are designed for low-energy impacts and would fail in this scenario"],a:3,e:"PPE is the last line of defence and has limits."},
{z:"Dropped Objects",q:"What is the difference between a 'static' and 'dynamic' dropped object?",o:["Static drops happen only during night shifts; dynamic drops happen only during day shifts","Static drops happen only in offshore maritime locations; dynamic drops happen only on land","There is no real difference between the two terms in standard safety hazard categorisation","Static drops are fixtures failing under gravity; dynamic drops are items lost during work"],a:3,e:"Static: loose items, fixtures. Dynamic: tools or loads dropped during work or movement."},
{z:"Dropped Objects",q:"Why is secondary retention used on light fittings and signs offshore?",o:["It provides a secondary restraint in case intense vibration destroys the primary mounting","It improves the overall visual appearance and symmetry of the whole offshore installation","It decreases the overall weight of the electrical fixture attached to the superstructure","It improves the electrical grounding path and lowers the unit's overall power consumption"],a:0,e:"Secondary retention provides a backup if the primary fixing fails."},
{z:"Dropped Objects",q:"You must work at height with a heavy wrench. What is best practice?",o:["Place the tool loosely in the open front pocket of your fire-resistant work overalls","Tether the tool to a certified harness anchor and barricade an exclusion zone below","Throw the tool down to a waiting colleague stationed on the deck below immediately","Fasten the tool to the exterior surface of your safety helmet using industrial tape"],a:1,e:"Tethers prevent the drop, and barricades protect if something else falls."},
{z:"Dropped Objects",q:"Scaffolders are building a platform above a walkway. Which is the best control?",o:["Tell everyone passing beneath to walk faster while the worksite is active above","Place a laminated warning sign on a portable stanchion by the walkway entrance","Install toe boards and debris netting, plus a sturdy barricade at ground level","Tell the scaffolding team to shout loud alerts to pedestrians walking down below"],a:2,e:"Physical controls and exclusion zones beat shouting."},
{z:"Mechanical Hazards",q:"You are using an angle grinder. Where should you stand relative to the disc?",o:["Position yourself in line with the rotating disc, for best visual alignment while cutting","Position yourself safely outside the plane of rotation, with the guard facing towards you","Position yourself freely with the guard removed, to allow full movement during the work","Position yourself facing the cutting edge of the spinning disc at maximum arm's length"],a:1,e:"If a disc bursts, fragments fly along its plane of rotation."},
{z:"Mechanical Hazards",q:"A cold chisel has a mushroomed head. Why is it dangerous?",o:["The deformation makes the tool notably heavier and harder to manoeuvre securely during use","The deformed striking surface removes the tool's capacity to cut through the target metal","The repeated impacts slowly and visibly change the colour of the adjacent metal structure","The deformed overhangs can break off under impact and fly outward like high-speed shrapnel"],a:3,e:"Mushroomed heads fragment. Dress or replace the tool."},
{z:"Mechanical Hazards",q:"A chain block or ratchet lever hoist is being used to pull a load, and the load suddenly releases. What is the line-of-fire hazard?",o:["The tensioned operating lever can whip backward wildly and strike anyone within its arc","The violent release of stored energy irreversibly changes the colour of the steel chain","The loss of tension makes the steel chain links increase dramatically in overall weight","The whole lifting mechanism goes fully silent and stops all of its operational function"],a:0,e:"When tension releases suddenly, the lever swings rapidly."},
{z:"Mechanical Hazards",q:"A winch drum is rotating with wire on it. A colleague wants to guide the wire onto the drum with his hand. What is correct?",o:["This is acceptable provided the worker is always wearing rated thick leather work gloves","This is acceptable provided the winch drum is revolving at a slow, controlled velocity","Never guide tensioned wire onto a rotating drum by hand; use powered spooling equipment","This is acceptable provided the physical size of the winch mechanism is relatively small"],a:2,e:"Hands and gloves can be pulled into the drum in an instant."},
{z:"Marine & Emergency",q:"A helicopter has landed with rotors turning. How should passengers approach it?",o:["Approach from the rear quadrant to reduce your exposure to the engine exhaust heat","Run towards the aft section to retrieve heavy luggage near the spinning tail rotor","Approach from any handy angle once the main turbine engines have fully started up","Approach solely when signalled, stay in the pilot's view, and avoid the tail rotor"],a:3,e:"Tail rotors are nearly invisible and fatal. Follow signals and approach as briefed."},
{z:"Marine & Emergency",q:"Loose items such as hats and plastic sheets are on the helideck. Why are they a concern?",o:["Rotor wash can accelerate them into engines or at personnel, causing severe injury","They make the helicopter landing zone look disordered and untidy to incoming crews","They create a considerable slip hazard when weather brings rainwater across the deck","They are not a real safety risk during normal offshore aviation flight operations"],a:0,e:"Foreign object debris is a major hazard on a helideck."},
{z:"Marine & Emergency",q:"After disembarking, you are tempted to take a photo at the helideck while the rotors are still turning. What is right?",o:["Take your photos while standing near the front of the aircraft nose section","Go straight to the designated safe zone as instructed by the landing officer","Stay still on the outside perimeter of the helideck to capture the landscape","Advance closer to the spinning tail rotor to obtain a superior camera angle"],a:1,e:"Never linger near rotors."},
{z:"Marine & Emergency",q:"A watertight door on a platform is being closed in heavy wind. Where should your hand be?",o:["Placed on the outermost swinging edge of the door, dragging it towards the doorway","Placed on the inner pivot hinge mechanism to gain leverage while closing the door","Placed on the operating handle, with fingers kept clear of the closing door edges","Placed on the stationary outer door frame to support your body weight against wind"],a:2,e:"Wind or vessel motion can slam doors shut and amputate fingers."},
{z:"Marine & Emergency",q:"During a fast rescue craft or lifeboat drill, what is correct regarding the hook release gear?",o:["Activate the release mechanism early to improve the overall deployment time of the drill","Keep a continuous grip on the release handle while seated within the suspended lifeboat","Never use or place hands near the release gear unless authorised, trained, and waterborne","Engage the release mechanism while the craft still remains suspended from the steel davit"],a:2,e:"Premature release has caused fatal falls."},
{z:"Marine & Emergency",q:"The general alarm sounds while a crane lift is in progress. What is the safest expectation?",o:["The lift is suspended and secured so that all emergency muster routes remain unobstructed","The lift continues without interruption because the lifting task is close to its conclusion","The banksman directs the heavy suspended load over the main emergency walking muster route","Personnel in transit walk beneath the suspended load to reach the assigned muster station"],a:0,e:"Muster routes must never pass under suspended loads."},
{z:"Marine & Emergency",q:"A gas alarm sounds and you hear a hissing release. Which action best reduces line-of-fire exposure?",o:["Walk towards the source of the hissing to visually identify the leakage location","Move straight downwind of the escaping vapour plume to examine the release origin","Remain in place and utilise your cellular phone to record the complete incident","Move crosswind and upwind, avoid the release path, and safely proceed to muster"],a:3,e:"Jet releases follow a line. Move out of it and upwind."},
{z:"Marine & Emergency",q:"A storm increases the vessel's movement. What is the line-of-fire implication for loose items on deck?",o:["There is no implication since deck equipment has significant engineered base weight","Items can slide or become missiles; secure everything and halt non-essential work","All heavy equipment remains in its designated storage location without any shifting","Solely the heaviest, most cumbersome machinery could shift in a hazardous fashion"],a:1,e:"Motion converts loose items into moving hazards."},
{z:"Behaviours & Stop Work",q:"You see a colleague standing under a suspended load, and the lift supervisor is busy. What should you do?",o:["Wait until the lift supervisor ends their task before raising the noticed safety issue","Use your stop work authority, halt the operation, and clear the colleague from danger","Say nothing because the colleague is experienced and has done these lifts many times","Use your mobile phone to take clear photographic evidence of the safety policy breach"],a:1,e:"Everyone has the authority and responsibility to stop work."},
{z:"Behaviours & Stop Work",q:"The permit says 'area isolated and barricaded', but you can see two people working inside the barricade. What should you do?",o:["Assume the final permit paperwork is accurate and disregard what you can actually see","Enter the cordoned-off exclusion zone and join the personnel inside to assist with it","Halt the operation now and challenge the permit mismatch before any more work resumes","Continue with your unconnected work because you are not involved in that working party"],a:2,e:"If reality does not match the paperwork, the paper is wrong."},
{z:"Behaviours & Stop Work",q:"A crane lift is planned over a laydown area where scaffolders are working. What is the right response?",o:["Spot the SIMOPS conflict, halt work, and coordinate a safe exclusion zone via permits","Tell the scaffolding team to stay extremely vigilant while the crane lift is happening","Complete the overhead lift as fast as possible before the scaffolders notice the load","Continue with the lift but request the banksman to shout continuous loud warnings below"],a:0,e:"Simultaneous operations need explicit coordination."},
{z:"Behaviours & Stop Work",q:"Which set of questions best helps identify line-of-fire hazards before starting a task?",o:["How long will the activity take, who is supervising, and when is the lunch break?","Who is the project manager, what is the weather forecast, and what is the cost?","Where is the toolbox, who holds the padlock keys, and what is the permit number?","What hazardous energy is present, where could it go, and where will my body be?"],a:3,e:"Energy, direction, position, the three keys to line-of-fire awareness."},
{z:"Behaviours & Stop Work",q:"Night shift hands over to day shift without mentioning that a pressure test is under way in a barricaded area. What is the risk?",o:["The incoming shift may blindly send people right into the dangerous exclusion zone","The pressure integrity test will require much more time to complete than scheduled","The pressure integrity test will finish much sooner than the first planned schedule","There is no danger because the barricades guarantee the protection of all personnel"],a:0,e:"Handover is a critical barrier. Residual hazards must be communicated."},
{z:"Behaviours & Stop Work",q:"It is day 13 of a 14-day swing and you are on a 12-hour night shift. What best describes the line-of-fire implications?",o:["Fatigue has no measurable effect on hazard recognition throughout operations at all","Experienced personnel are naturally immune to the effects of accumulated deep fatigue","Fatigue dulls awareness and reflex time, demanding extra caution with body placement","Fatigue only affects administrative personnel who work inside the main office block"],a:2,e:"Fatigue erodes situational awareness."},
{z:"Behaviours & Stop Work",q:"A senior colleague says, 'I have done this a thousand times, just stand there.' Where he points is in the line of fire. What is the right response?",o:["Stand where directed because the veteran colleague has far more field experience than you","Respectfully use stop work authority, refuse the position, and ask for the task re-planned","Stand where directed but stay extremely vigilant and watch the area around you continuously","Stand where directed, provided you also wear extra rated protective safety equipment on site"],a:1,e:"Experience does not remove the hazard. Challenge respectfully."},
{z:"Behaviours & Stop Work",q:"Which control is MOST effective against a line-of-fire hazard?",o:["Installing many high-visibility warning signs around the border of the hazard zone","Delivering a thorough verbal briefing so personnel know the specific hazard details","Issuing thick padded clothing to shield personnel from the likely physical impact","Engineering the hazard out completely or relocating personnel from the danger zone"],a:3,e:"Top of the hierarchy: eliminate or engineer people out of the risk."},
{z:"Behaviours & Stop Work",q:"Orange barrier tape is placed around an area under a crane lift. What does the tape do?",o:["It functions as a strong physical net that stops falling objects striking people","It provides only a visual warning and relies on personnel staying out voluntarily","It gives total physical protection against any sudden fast release of trapped gas","It converts the surrounding area into a fully risk-free and protected working zone"],a:1,e:"Tape is a visual cue only. It controls access by behaviour, not by force."},
{z:"Behaviours & Stop Work",q:"A bolt dropped from a platform lands on the deck and nobody is injured. How should it be classified and handled?",o:["It should be ignored and excluded from safety records because nobody was injured","It should be addressed informally once the dropped bolt is cleared away safely","It must be documented, reviewed, and rated as a high-potential near miss event","It should be documented in the database solely if an identical incident repeats"],a:2,e:"Potential severity matters, not outcome."},
{z:"Behaviours & Stop Work",q:"When observing a task, you see someone in the line of fire. What is the best intervention style?",o:["Stop the activity, hold a respectful dialogue, and agree on a secure position","Observe quietly and document the violation in a report at the shift conclusion","Shout at the person to shame them publicly and force their instant compliance","Say nothing and overlook the situation to prevent any conflict with the person"],a:0,e:"Positive intervention at the time fixes the risk and builds trust."},
{z:"Behaviours & Stop Work",q:"Which principle underlies all line-of-fire controls?",o:["Fast reflexes will surely protect you from all sudden unexpected machine failures","Certified new protective equipment makes you immune to all known physical hazards","Shift supervisors can constantly monitor every single employee on the active site","Human reflexes are too sluggish; personnel must be separated from hazardous zones"],a:3,e:"Human reaction time is far too slow compared to released energy."}
];
